{"author":{"id":"973fc03426eba9a243913e0f6e6823e102397744f60a9ab11c882af948c310c4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1778941326,"nonce":"5mdgzqXQ+Id6kL/k4gXZTq58uGk=","title":"Ledger core: schema, project identity, events, outbox, backups","message":"## Parent\nUmbrella: 097d2b7 — Mandible canonical issue/work ledger with git-bug import/export.\n\n## Scope\nBuild the durable local ledger foundation behind existing behavior.\n\nThis issue owns:\n- sequential `src/db.ts` migrations for canonical ledger tables;\n- Mandible-owned canonical IDs for issues/comments/events/links;\n- logical project/repo identity separate from local checkout path;\n- `src/ledger/` service boundary and typed command API;\n- current-state tables plus append-only `ledger_events`;\n- adapter outbox table/status model;\n- backup/export coverage for all non-reproducible ledger data;\n- SQLite referential integrity policy for ledger tables;\n- unit tests for schema, migrations, IDs, project identity, event writes, and outbox writes.\n\n## Design constraints\nSQLite is the local storage engine, not the conceptual backend. All user-visible mutations must go through ledger commands that write current state, append an event, enqueue any needed outbox record, and return data suitable for API/SSE publication.\n\nCanonical IDs must be globally stable and must not derive from git-bug short IDs, local paths, branches, or worktree names. Use an `iss_`, `com_`, `evt_`, `rel_` prefix family with UUIDv7-equivalent or documented timestamp-plus-random IDs.\n\nProject identity must distinguish:\n- logical project / remote identity;\n- local checkout/worktree paths;\n- forks that continue upstream issue identity;\n- forks that intentionally start a separate issue namespace.\n\n## Out of scope\n- Switching issue routes or CLI reads/writes to ledger.\n- git-bug import/export implementation beyond adapter interfaces and outbox contracts.\n- graph/UI rewrites.\n\n## Verification\n- Fresh DB migration test.\n- Upgrade migration test from current schema.\n- ID generation tests for prefix, uniqueness, sort behavior if promised, and non-path derivation.\n- Ledger command tests proving current-state rows, events, and outbox rows are written transactionally.\n- Backup test or script update proving ledger tables are included.\n- Referential integrity tests for canonical issue/comment/link relationships.\n\n## Done means\nThe ledger core can exist safely in shadow mode with no behavior cutover. Existing Mandible issue/git-bug behavior continues to work, while tests prove the new ledger can record canonical issue/comment/relationship events and survive backup/restore coverage.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}